WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.701
-Welcome, welcome, welcome! -Thank you, thank you.

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.400
Finally, Jimmy Fallon.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.901
-I mean, I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:08.167
I -- You know, I saw you. I met you once before.

00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:09.868
I don't know if you remember this.

00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:11.567
You did a duet with Michael Bublé.

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:14.534
-Yeah, a subtle little number with an unknown singer

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:15.934
called Michael Bublé. -Yeah.

00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:18.534
And you ripped the roof off the place!

00:00:18.534 --> 00:00:20.200
You were phenomenal!

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.400
I'm like, "You can sing?!"

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.667
-That's my proper job. -I know --

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:26.701
But you can sing, by the way.

00:00:26.701 --> 00:00:28.968
Holy moly. You're fantastic. -Thanks. Thanks, pal. Thanks.

00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:31.934
-How did you get that talent? Was it mom or dad or...?

00:00:31.934 --> 00:00:35.367
-Yeah, I mean, I kind of come from a long line of...

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:36.767
♪ Opera singers ♪

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:38.400
-Oh, really? -Yes.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.000
Yes. -An opera singer?

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.267
-My mom -- Yeah, my mom was a principal

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:42.868
at Covent Garden before I was born.

00:00:42.868 --> 00:00:44.501
Royal Opera House. -Wow!

00:00:44.501 --> 00:00:46.133
-And then she's been at the London Coliseum

00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:47.501
for like 30 years.

00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:49.734
Both her parents were also opera singers.

00:00:49.734 --> 00:00:53.734
So whenever my mom has come to see me do a West End show,

00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:57.033
she'd be like, "Are you looking after your voice?"

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:58.634
-Yeah. Wow.

00:00:58.634 --> 00:00:59.901
-"It sounds like you're shouting."

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:01.701
-Wow. What a talented family. -Yeah, yeah.

00:01:01.701 --> 00:01:03.634
-Did you think every family was this talented?

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:05.767
Like, would you go over friends' houses and be like,

00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:06.968
"Your parents don't sing?"

00:01:06.968 --> 00:01:08.534
-Yeah. "Your parents go to and do what?"

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:11.133
-Exactly. Yeah. -They -- When I was little, I --

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:12.801
Literally, like, from the age of eight,

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:15.667
I would be there sitting in the auditorium and theaters

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:17.767
and just, like, soaking it all up.

00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:19.467
And I genuinely thought

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:21.567
that all people's parents worked in the theater.

00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:22.934
-Yeah. -Because I had, like,

00:01:22.934 --> 00:01:24.367
no frame of reference, really, you know?

00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:26.234
-You just lived -- grew up in the theater. That was it.

00:01:26.234 --> 00:01:27.901
-Yes, and be, like, around in the dressing rooms

00:01:27.901 --> 00:01:30.267
and saying hello to, like, the attendants in the toilet.

00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:31.434
They'd be like, "Oh, hi, Hannah."

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:32.901
-No way. -Yeah, just wandering about.

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:34.934
-So you really lived in there. -Because it was that era

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:37.767
where you just could, so you did, you know?

00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:39.534
Like going up to the dressing room.

00:01:39.534 --> 00:01:40.834
And -- Yeah. Just beautiful times.

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:42.634
-So, are you kind of -- I mean, there's two things

00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:44.501
that I want to talk to you about -- Or three things.

00:01:44.501 --> 00:01:46.567
But you're hosting the Olivier Awards,

00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:48.767
which is the biggest -- -No pressure. Yeah.

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.133
It's like our Tonys. -Exactly.

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:53.400
-Yeah, and I'm always getting on my soap box about

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.801
how people in theater, around the world,

00:01:55.801 --> 00:01:57.767
should be able to screen more, you know?

00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:00.501
Because they are the ones that really, really want to do it.

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:02.033
Because it's not for the money. -Yeah.

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:03.634
-They just want to play great roles.

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:05.534
-Yeah. -I think -- I've been so vocal,

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:07.801
they're just like, "Yeah, get her. She's gobby."

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:09.334
-Yeah. [ Laughter ]

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:11.501
-I just thought of that -- Yeah, you are good.

00:02:11.501 --> 00:02:13.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:17.701
But then you're also co-hosting the Eurovision Song Contest.

00:02:17.701 --> 00:02:19.200
-I mean!

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:22.100
Do you get it here?

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.968
Do you get the Eurovision Song Contest here?

00:02:23.968 --> 00:02:25.701
-We don't really, but we know what it is.

00:02:25.701 --> 00:02:27.734
-It is an event, man. It is an event.

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:30.467
-No, it's massive. The world watches it.

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:32.467
Everyone but America watches it. It's, like, very interesting.

00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:34.801
-Yeah, it's insane. And all the countries,

00:02:34.801 --> 00:02:36.467
they're an absolute bunch of lunatics.

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:37.601
-Yeah.

00:02:37.601 --> 00:02:39.033
-The songs that they come out with, I'm like,

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:41.634
"Wow. That outfit looked like

00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:43.767
a good idea in the mirror, I think."

00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:45.834
-Yeah. But it's a big deal. But I'm like --

00:02:45.834 --> 00:02:48.334
I want to say ABBA was -- -Yeah, they were discovered.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.767
-Discovered, right? -Right.

00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:51.501
-From -- From this contest. -Right.

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:53.200
-So it's a lot of pressure on you.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.434
-But I'm so chuffed because Graham Norton and I --

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:56.968
Who I know we both love.

00:02:56.968 --> 00:02:58.300
-I love Graham Norton. -Yeah.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.100
He and I are doing the final, you know,

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.000
"Good evening, Portugal!"

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.501
-Wow! -You know?

00:03:03.501 --> 00:03:04.601
-Are you excited?!

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:06.033
-The France... [ Speaking French ]

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:07.834
Yeah! My God. I can't wait.

00:03:07.834 --> 00:03:11.501
-Let's talk about "Ted Lasso." Season three. You're phenomenal.

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:12.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:12.634 --> 00:03:14.400
You are phenomenal in this. -Thank you.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.100
-And season three came out.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.133
And I saw -- Jason was talking about it.

00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:19.501
And people are kind of assuming

00:03:19.501 --> 00:03:21.367
it might be, maybe, the final season.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:22.567
We don't know. -No, no.

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:24.934
What he said -- it's like the natural end

00:03:24.934 --> 00:03:26.934
of a three-season arc. -That --

00:03:26.934 --> 00:03:30.367
-So there was a beginning, a middle, and an end of this.

00:03:30.367 --> 00:03:32.734
We don't even know. I -- Do you know what's really good

00:03:32.734 --> 00:03:34.100
that I genuinely don't know?

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.801
Because I know that otherwise I'll be like...

00:03:35.801 --> 00:03:37.567
[ Speaks gibberish ] "Whoopsie daisy!"

00:03:37.567 --> 00:03:39.501
-So it might not be the final -- -It might not be.

00:03:39.501 --> 00:03:40.868
-Oh, okay. Good. -We don't know.

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:42.501
We genuinely don't know. -I didn't know this.

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:44.100
-I don't think Jason even knows, to be fair.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.033
I think he needs to be in a dark room and just go...

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:49.434
-Yeah, yeah. Yeah. -Just for a minute, you know?

00:03:49.434 --> 00:03:51.100
-Everyone loves it.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.234
And, gosh, it came at a time when we all needed it.

00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:55.167
-Oh, God, yeah. -Because it was positive.

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.300
And it was just so great. -Yeah.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.901
-And, honestly, it was like chicken soup for us Americans.

00:03:59.901 --> 00:04:02.400
-Those writers -- That writers' room, man.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.601
-Yeah. -They have supplied

00:04:03.601 --> 00:04:05.834
chicken soup for the world. -I agree. Yeah.

00:04:05.834 --> 00:04:09.701
That's a good -- I totally agree.

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:12.133
Being in it, do you --

00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:14.501
do you love Ted as much as everyone loves Ted?

00:04:14.501 --> 00:04:16.567
And, like, do you hear him, like, when you're doing things?

00:04:16.567 --> 00:04:18.467
You're like, "Oh, yeah. Ted Lasso would do..."

00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:21.601
-Yeah, well, do you know, I kind of have always prided myself

00:04:21.601 --> 00:04:23.667
on being a little bit like that anyway.

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:27.000
And I was thrilled when the show was such a success

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.334
because it was celebrating kindness and being funny

00:04:30.334 --> 00:04:32.267
rather than just roasting everyone

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:34.200
and being snippy and bitchy... and funny, you know?

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.634
-Yeah, yeah. -So I kind of loved it.

00:04:35.634 --> 00:04:37.167
But the thing for me has always been --

00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:39.467
You know his line when he's in the locker room and he just

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:41.367
goes, "Onward, forward"?

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:44.601
I love that because you need to move forward from the bad times

00:04:44.601 --> 00:04:47.367
but also not get caught up in yourself in the good times...

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:48.634
-Yep. -...as well.

00:04:48.634 --> 00:04:50.701
-"Onward, forward." -So, onward, forward always.

00:04:50.701 --> 00:04:53.501
I love that. It always -- It always kind of --

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:54.868
-Sticks in your head. -Stays with me. Yeah.

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:56.601
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:04:56.601 --> 00:05:00.100
Here's Hannah Waddingham and Juno Temple in "Ted Lasso."

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.667
Take a look at this. -Oh, my girl. My girl.

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:04.434
-Rupert must just be so happy right now.

00:05:04.434 --> 00:05:06.767
-He's really gotten stuck in your head these days, huh?

00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:08.200
-No!

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.400
I mean, yes, but he's not stuck

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.167
in the same way he used to be stuck.

00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:16.267
I mean, back then, I wanted to destroy everything

00:05:16.267 --> 00:05:18.901
that Rupert loved and owned and coveted.

00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:20.567
-Ultimate gangster move.

00:05:20.567 --> 00:05:21.934
-Legend.

00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:24.767
And, admittedly, that was me back then.

00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:29.567
But the me now doesn't feel the need to destroy Rupert's life.

00:05:29.567 --> 00:05:33.067
No, the now-me just wants to beat him, to win.

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:36.534
That's growth, right? -Sure.

00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:40.968
-Yeah! Hannah Waddingham, everybody!

00:05:40.968 --> 00:05:42.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.167
Season three of "Ted Lasso"

00:05:44.167 --> 00:05:47.067
premieres Wednesday on Apple TV Plus.

00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:49.300
We'll be right back with a performance from TWICE!

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.300
Stick around!

